hints_text:
Actually I'm not sure its possible to fix. Any middleware you apply run before the router sees the request. So if the router added the matched pattern to a request extension middleware wouldn't see the extension because they run _before_ the router. You'd only be able to see the extension in handlers but that seems less useful.

@gsson Is this something other rust web frameworks have support for?
I don't know, I haven't tried that many -- I know it's quite common frameworks for other languages though.

For me the primary driver is to be able to deploy Rust services that resembles our other (mostly Spring) Java services from the outside so we can do drop-in replacements where we want what Rust offers.

That said, what I'm looking for is a way of doing the telemetry/reporting the metrics/writing the logs, not necessarily writing middlewares. If there's some other way that's more than ok!

It would be nice to be able to do it once for a "stack" of routes, but if I have to wrap my routing in a few layers (one for each route, one for each method in each route and one to gather it and instrument each handler?) I guess...I guess I'll go write some macros :) 

I believe I've found a workaround. Though I first want apologies for the crazy types. If/when this becomes built in to axum we'll make sure to have a simpler API. But yeah this seems to work:

```rust
use axum::{
    body::{box_body, BoxBody},
    extract::Path,
    handler::get,
    http::Request,
    routing::Route,
    AddExtension, AddExtensionLayer, Router,
};
use std::sync::Arc;
use tower::ServiceBuilder;
use tower_http::{
    classify::{ServerErrorsAsFailures, SharedClassifier},
    map_response_body::{MapResponseBody, MapResponseBodyLayer},
    trace::{MakeSpan, Trace, TraceLayer},
};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Set the RUST_LOG, if it hasn't been explicitly defined
    if std::env::var_os("RUST_LOG").is_none() {
        std::env::set_var("RUST_LOG", "example_testing=debug,tower_http=debug")
    }
    tracing_subscriber::fmt::init();

    let app = Router::new().route_with_path_tracing(
        "/users/:id",
        get(|Path(id): Path<String>| async move {
            tracing::debug!(%id);
        }),
    );

    let addr = std::net::SocketAddr::from(([127, 0, 0, 1], 3000));
    axum::Server::bind(&addr)
        .serve(app.into_make_service())
        .await
        .unwrap();
}

trait RouterExt<S> {
    fn route_with_path_tracing<T>(self, path: &str, svc: T) -> Router<RouteWithPathTracing<T, S>>;
}

impl<S> RouterExt<S> for Router<S> {
    fn route_with_path_tracing<T>(self, path: &str, svc: T) -> Router<RouteWithPathTracing<T, S>> {
        let path: Arc<str> = path.into();
        let svc = ServiceBuilder::new()
            .layer(MapResponseBodyLayer::new(box_body as _))
            .layer(AddExtensionLayer::new(RoutePath(path.clone())))
            .layer(TraceLayer::new_for_http().make_span_with(MakeSpanFromPath))
            .service(svc);
        self.route(&path, svc)
    }
}

type RouteWithPathTracing<T, S> = Route<
    MapResponseBody<
        AddExtension<
            Trace<T, SharedClassifier<ServerErrorsAsFailures>, MakeSpanFromPath>,
            RoutePath,
        >,
        fn(TraceBody) -> BoxBody,
    >,
    S,
>;

type TraceBody = tower_http::trace::ResponseBody<
    BoxBody,
    tower_http::classify::NeverClassifyEos<tower_http::classify::ServerErrorsFailureClass>,
    tower_http::trace::DefaultOnBodyChunk,
    tower_http::trace::DefaultOnEos,
    tower_http::trace::DefaultOnFailure,
>;

#[derive(Clone)]
struct RoutePath(Arc<str>);

#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
struct MakeSpanFromPath;

impl<B> MakeSpan<B> for MakeSpanFromPath {
    fn make_span(&mut self, request: &Request<B>) -> tracing::Span {
        let RoutePath(path) = request.extensions().get::<RoutePath>().unwrap();
        tracing::debug_span!(
            "request",
            method = %request.method(),
            path = %path,
        )
    }
}
```

That results in logs like this:

```
Oct 14 23:42:57.684 DEBUG request{method=GET path=/users/:id}: tower_http::trace::on_request: started processing request
Oct 14 23:42:57.685 DEBUG request{method=GET path=/users/:id}: example_testing: id=123
Oct 14 23:42:57.685 DEBUG request{method=GET path=/users/:id}: tower_http::trace::on_response: finished processing request latency=1 ms status=200
```
What a beauty! :)

Thanks! I think I can build something from that

